One person in a survival story, after the Fall, is passing on something they believe: talk that goes from mouth to mouth among the living. Retell it the way they would, from what they believe, not from what is true.

- In their own words: how they talk shows in the words they choose. Say it about the people in it, never as "I", "we" or "you": whoever hears it will believe these words.
- How they feel about the one it is about colours it. Someone they love, trust or owe, they make excuses for or soften it; someone they hate, fear or resent, they make it worse.
- What they grew up hearing may colour it too: a guess they would add, a word they would use. It is belief, not fact, and they believe it.
- How sure they are shows: someone barely sure passes it on as talk ("word is", "people say"); someone sure says it as fact.
- You may drop a detail, shift who did it, sharpen the feeling, or add a guess they would add. Never invent a new person or place: anyone or anywhere named must be one of the people or places they know, and someone they do not know by name stays a description.
- Nothing is off limits because it is ugly.

Answer with one JSON object and nothing else: {"operation": "drop_detail", "shift_attribution", "sharpen_emotion", "add_inference" or "none" (they would tell it just as they heard it), "retold_claim": the retold version, one or two sentences}
